---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

// Publication status is intentionally a non-clickable "Coming Soon on Amazon" tag.
// TO PUBLISH when a live listing exists: replace the .status-tag <span> in the
// pub-status block below with an anchor to the real Amazon product URL
// (open in a new tab, rel="noopener") and change the copy to "Available Now".
// No ASIN or Amazon URL is emitted to the browser until then.
---

<Base
  title="The Mycelium of Meaning — Thought Partners by Dale Joseph"
  description="The Mycelium of Meaning by Dale Joseph. A living-systems history of machine language — from rule-based systems to transformers and agents — and what their successes and failures reveal about syntax, meaning, and human responsibility. Coming soon on Amazon."
>
  <Header slot="header" />

  <!-- BOOK INTRO -->
  <section class="section book-intro">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="label gold">Recent Work</p>
      <h1>The Mycelium of Meaning</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">How Language Models Grow, Fail, and Reshape the Question of Understanding</p>

      <!-- Publication status: non-clickable until a live Amazon listing exists.
           See the frontmatter note above for how to add the real buy link. -->
      <p class="pub-status" aria-label="Publication status">
        <span class="status-tag">Coming Soon on Amazon</span>
      </p>

      <div class="divider"></div>

      <div class="book-desc">
        <p>
          Artificial intelligence did not arrive fully formed. It grew. For more than seventy years,
          researchers have tried to teach machines how language works — and each attempt solved a real
          problem while exposing another boundary. <em>The Mycelium of Meaning</em> traces that history
          and asks a harder question than whether machines are becoming intelligent:
          <strong>what do their successes and failures reveal about language, comprehension, and us?</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a book about the development of machine language systems — from rule-based and
          statistical methods through neural networks and transformers to retrieval, agents, and
          the systems that surround them. It reads that history as a way of thinking clearly about a
          set of distinctions that keep getting blurred.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE CENTRAL INQUIRY -->
  <section class="section section--dark inquiry">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <h2>The Central Inquiry</h2>
      <p class="inquiry-intro">
        The book's subject is not "AI" in the generic sense. It is a sustained inquiry into the
        difference between what these systems do and what we are tempted to believe they do — the
        distinctions between:
      </p>
      <ul class="distinctions">
        <li><strong>syntax</strong> and <strong>meaning</strong></li>
        <li><strong>probability</strong> and <strong>understanding</strong></li>
        <li><strong>representation</strong> and <strong>truth</strong></li>
        <li><strong>machine capability</strong> and <strong>human intent</strong></li>
        <li><strong>intelligence</strong> and <strong>relationship</strong></li>
        <li><strong>simulation</strong> and <strong>lived meaning</strong></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE METAPHOR -->
  <section class="section metaphor">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <h2>Why Mycelium</h2>
      <p>
        The governing metaphor is the <strong>mycelium</strong> — the underground network of fungal
        threads that connects a forest. It is the book's way of holding a claim about meaning itself:
        that meaning is relational, distributed, contextual, and alive — generated through connection
        rather than contained in isolated symbols.
      </p>
      <p>
        A rule-based system captured syntax but not meaning. A statistical system predicted linguistic
        habits without establishing understanding. A neural network mapped language into geometry
        without turning coherence into truth. Each era achieved something genuine, and each mistook a
        necessary part for the sufficient whole. The mycelium is what remains: not a single symbol that
        carries meaning, but the living web of context, use, and consequence in which meaning grows.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        Meaning emerges through relationships of context, use, interpretation, and consequence —
        while responsibility remains with those who design, deploy, evaluate, and live with these systems.
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE ARGUMENT -->
  <section class="section section--dark argument">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <h2>Symbiosis, Not Replacement</h2>
      <p>
        <em>The Mycelium of Meaning</em> ultimately argues for a human–machine relationship built on
        <strong>symbiosis rather than replacement</strong>. Distinct intelligences can produce meaning
        together without becoming the same thing. But symbiosis is not surrender: human intention,
        judgment, responsibility, and meaning-making remain central.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is neither a prophecy of machine consciousness nor a dismissal of what these systems can do.
        It is a disciplined account of capability without mythology, collaboration without sameness, and
        responsibility without the illusion of complete control.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SAMPLE CTA -->
  <section class="section sample-cta">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <h2>Read a Sample</h2>
      <p>
        Chapter&nbsp;1, <em>The Mechanical Dream</em>, follows the rule-based era — from the 1954
        Georgetown–IBM translation demonstration to ELIZA and SHRDLU — and arrives at the axiom the
        rest of the book stands on: <strong>meaning cannot be reduced to syntax.</strong>
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href="/mycelium-of-meaning/sample" class="btn btn--filled">Read Chapter 1 &rarr;</a>
        <a href="/blog" class="btn">Essays</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer slot="footer" />
</Base>

<style>
  .label {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .book-intro h1 {
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .subtitle {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .pub-status {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .status-tag {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    border: 1px solid var(--gold);
    color: var(--gold);
    padding: 0.3rem 0.75rem;
  }

  .book-desc {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  .book-desc p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .section--dark {
    background: var(--bg-elevated);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .inquiry h2,
  .metaphor h2,
  .argument h2,
  .sample-cta h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .inquiry-intro {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-style: italic;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    max-width: 620px;
  }

  .distinctions {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
    gap: 0.75rem 2rem;
  }

  .distinctions li {
    padding: 0.75rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 1.02rem;
  }

  .distinctions strong {
    color: var(--gold-light);
  }

  .metaphor blockquote {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--gold);
    padding: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    color: var(--gold-light);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .distinctions {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
